Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> As I said, using "la" for:
>=20
>     ...when suddenly a White Rabbit with pink eyes ran close by her...
>=20
> is at the very least semantically odd. (Syntactically, it is fine.)

By English semantics it is somewhat odd.  It seems precisely to=20
represent in Lojban the choice in the English to capitalize what are=20
normally common nouns in English.  Compare "suddenly a John Smith with=20
brown hair ran past her" (would "la" be semantically odd in translating=20
that?).

With the proviso that I don't know what "la" is currently defined as=20
under the adopted xorlo %^)  The capitalization suggests a named=20
referent, and in English suggests the possibility of plural such named=20
referents of which this is but one.  Lojban "la" is (I suspect)=20
ambivalent in number.  It is possible that under xorlo, which IIRC has=20
no default number interpretation, all instances of English "a" should=20
correctly be translated as "pa lo" or "pa le" or "pa la".

